          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 Neural Net (9 pts)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.129,
            0.682,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.41,
            0.593,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": "Data points are: Negative: (­1, 0) (2, ­2) Positive: (1, 0)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.447,
            0.885,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "Recall that for neural nets, the negative class is represented by a desired output of 0 and the positive class by a desired output of 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.499,
            0.49,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "1. Assume we have a single sigmoid unit:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.378,
            0.52,
            0.627,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.719,
            0.885,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "Assume that the weights are $w _ { 0 } = 0 , w _ { 1 } = 1 , w _ { 2 } = 1$ . What is the computed y value for each of the points on the diagram above?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.77,
            0.702,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "(a) $x = ( - 1,0),   y = \\mathbf{s}(\\mathbf{0} \\cdot - \\mathbf{1} + \\mathbf{1} \\cdot - \\mathbf{1} + \\mathbf{1} \\cdot \\mathbf{0}) = \\mathbf{s}( - \\mathbf{1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\\mathbf{27}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.795,
            0.676,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "(b) x = (2, −2), y = s(0 · −1 + 1 · −2 + 1 · 2) = s(0) = 0.5"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.82,
            0.657,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": "x = (1, 0), y = \\mathbf {s} (\\mathbf {0} \\cdot - \\mathbf {1} + \\mathbf {1} \\cdot \\mathbf {1} + \\mathbf {1} \\cdot \\mathbf {0}) = \\mathbf {s} (\\mathbf {1}) = \\mathbf {0 . 7 3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.854,
            0.828,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "Hint: Some useful values of the sigmoid s(z) are $s ( - 1 ) = 0 . 2 7 { \\mathrm { ~ a n d ~ } } s ( 1 ) = 0 . 7 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.897,
            0.506,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": "6"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.092,
            0.886,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "2. What would be the change in $w _ { 2 }$ as determined by backpropagation using a step size $( \\eta )$ of 1.0? Assume that the input is $x = (2, -2)$ and the initial weights are as specified above. Show the formula you are using as well as the numerical result."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.163,
            0.269,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "(a) $\\Delta w _ { 2 } =$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.198,
            0.235,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "Solution:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.336,
            0.242,
            0.661,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{r c l} \\Delta w _ {2} & = & - \\eta \\frac {\\partial E}{\\partial w _ {2}} \\\\ & = & - \\eta \\frac {\\partial E}{\\partial y} \\frac {\\partial y}{\\partial z} \\frac {\\partial z}{\\partial w _ {2}} \\\\ & = & - \\eta (y - y ^ {i}) y (1 - y) x _ {2} \\\\ & = & (- 1) (0. 5 + 0) (0. 5) (0. 5) (- 2) \\\\ & = & 0. 2 5 \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.445,
            0.265,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": "Derivations:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.431,
            0.472,
            0.589,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": "E = \\frac {1}{2} (y - y ^ {i}) ^ {2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.513,
            0.538,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "y = s (z)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.539,
            0.566,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "z = \\sum_ {i = 0} ^ {2} w _ {i} x _ {i}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.587,
            0.554,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "\\frac {\\partial E}{\\partial y} = y - y ^ {i}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.422,
            0.632,
            0.57,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "\\frac {\\partial y}{\\partial z} = y (1 - y)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.674,
            0.523,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "\\frac {\\partial z}{\\partial w _ {2}} = x _ {2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.896,
            0.506,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "7"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.089,
            0.439,
            0.114
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5 Naive Bayes (8 pts)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.127,
            0.887,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "Consider a Naive Bayes problem with three features, $x _ { 1 } \\ldots x _ { 3 }$ . Imagine that we have seen a total of 12 training examples, 6 positive (with y = 1) and 6 negative (with $y = 0 )$ . Here is a table with some of the counts:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.185,
            0.352,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "|  | y = 0 | y = 1 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| x<sub>1</sub> = 1 | 6 | 6 |\n| x<sub>2</sub> = 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| x<sub>3</sub> = 1 | 2 | 4 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.276,
            0.795,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "1. Supply the following estimated probabilities. Use the Laplacian correction."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.305,
            0.442,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "\\bullet \\Pr (x _ {1} = 1 | y = 0) = \\frac {6 + 1}{6 + 2} = \\frac {7}{8}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.376,
            0.441,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "\\Pr (x _ {2} = 1 | y = 1) = \\frac {0 + 1}{6 + 2} = \\frac {1}{8}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.448,
            0.475,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": "\\bullet \\Pr (x _ {3} = 0 | y = 0) = 1 - \\frac {2 + 1}{6 + 2} = \\frac {5}{8}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.481,
            0.885,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": "2. Which feature plays the largest role in deciding the class of a new instance? Why? $x _ { 3 } ,$ because it has the biggest difference in the likelihood of being true for the two different classes. The other two features carry no information about the class."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.897,
            0.506,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 8,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.089,
            0.564,
            0.114
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6 Learning algorithms (16 pts)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.127,
            0.885,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "For each of the learning situations below, say what learning algorithm would be best to use, and why."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.179,
            0.885,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "1. You have about 1 million training examples in a 6­dimensional feature space. You only expect to be asked to classify 100 test examples."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.222,
            0.886,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "Nearest Neighbors is a good choice. The dimensionality is low and so appropriate for KNN. For KNN, training is very fast and since there are few classifications, the fact that this will be slow does not matter. With 1 million training examples, neural net and SVM will be extremely expensive to train. Naive Bayes is plausible on computational grounds but likely to be less accurate than KNN."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.344,
            0.886,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "2. You are going to develop a classifier to recommend which children should be assigned to special education classes in kindergarten. The classifier has to be justified to the board of education before it is implemented."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.405,
            0.885,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "A Decision Tree is a good choice since the resulting classifier will need to be understandable to humans."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.454,
            0.887,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "3. You are working for Am\\*z\\*n as it tries to take over the retailing world. You are trying to predict whether customer X will like a particular book, as a function of the input which is a vector of 1 million bits specifying whether each of Am\\*z\\*n’s other customers liked the book. You will train a classifier on a very large data set of books, where the inputs are everyone else’s preferences for that book, and the output is customer X’s preference for that book. The classifier will have to be updated frequently and efficiently as new data comes in."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.589,
            0.886,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "Naive Bayes is a good choice since it is fast to train and update. The dimensionality is high for Nearest Neighbors and Decision Trees. SVM’s have to be re­trained from scratch if the data changes. Neural Nets could be trained incrementally but it will generally take a lot of iterations to change the current settings of the weights."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.692,
            0.885,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": "4. You are trying to predict the average rainfall in California as a function of the measured currents and tides in the Pacific ocean in the previous six months."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.735,
            0.886,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "This is a regression problem; neural nets with linear output functions, regression trees or locally weighted nearest neighbors are all appropriate choices."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.896,
            0.506,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": "9"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.09,
            0.618,
            0.114
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7 Error versus complexity (15 pts)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.127,
            0.885,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": "Most learning algorithms we have seen try to find a hypotheses that minimizes error. But how do they attempt to control complexity? Here are some possible approaches:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.176,
            0.486,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "A: Use a fixed­complexity hypothesis class"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.207,
            0.596,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": "B: Include a complexity penalty in the measure of error"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.237,
            0.225,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "C: Nothing"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.268,
            0.886,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "For each of the following algorithms, specify which approach it uses and say what hypothesis class it uses (including any restrictions) and what complexity criterion (if any) is included in the measure of error. If the algorithm attempts to optimize the error measure, say whether it is guaranteed to find an optimal solution or just an approximation."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.359,
            0.259,
            0.376
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1. perceptron"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.382,
            0.885,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "A. It uses a fixed hypothesis class of linear separators. It is guaranteed to find a separator if one exists."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.431,
            0.265,
            0.449
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2. linear SVM"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.455,
            0.884,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "B. It includes a complexity penalty in the error criterion (which is to maximize the margin while separating the data). It optimizes this criterion."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.505,
            0.418,
            0.522
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3. decision tree with fixed depth"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.529,
            0.885,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "A. It uses a fixed hypothesis class, which is the class of fixed­depth trees. Implicitly, it tries to find the lowest­error tree within this class, but isn’t guaranteed to optimize that criterion."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.597,
            0.596,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "4. neural network (no weight decay or early stopping)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.621,
            0.885,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": "A. It uses a fixed hypothesis class, which is determined by the wiring diagram of the network."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.67,
            0.49,
            0.688
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5. SVM (with arbitrary data and $c < \\infty )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.695,
            0.884,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "B. It includes a complexity penalty in the error criterion (which is to maximize the margin subject to assigning an α < c to each data point."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.49,
            0.896,
            0.512,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "10"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.089,
            0.434,
            0.114
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8 Regression (12 pts)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.127,
            0.887,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "Consider a one­dimensional regression problem (predict y as a function of x). For each of the algorithms below, draw the approximate shape of the output of the algorithm, given the data points shown in the graph."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.197,
            0.568,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "1. 2­nearest­neighbor (equally weighted averaging)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.225,
            0.689,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
