    },
    {
      "page_idx": 12,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.088,
            0.239,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": "9 SVM"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.122,
            0.682,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.403,
                0.593,
                0.422
              ],
              "content": "Data points are: Negative: (­1, 0) (2, ­2) Positive: (1, 0)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.44,
            0.885,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "Recall that for SVMs, the negative class is represented by a desired output of ­1 and the positive class by a desired output of 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.493,
            0.886,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": "1. For each of the following separators (for the data shown above), indicate whether they satisfy all the conditions required for a support vector machine, assuming a linear kernel. Justify your answers very briefly."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.564,
            0.308,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "(a) $x _ { 1 } + x _ { 2 } = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.585,
            0.828,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": "Goes through the (2,­2) point so obviously not maximal margin."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.609,
            0.332,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "(b) $x _ { 1 } + 1 . 5 x _ { 2 } = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.631,
            0.783,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": "Yes. All three points are support vectors, with margin = 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.655,
            0.317,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "(c) $x _ { 1 } + 2 x _ { 2 } = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.677,
            0.884,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "No. Three points are needed to define a line, with two support vectors there is no unique maximal margin line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.72,
            0.327,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": "$2 x _ { 1 } + 3 x _ { 2 } = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.741,
            0.608,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "No. The margin for the points is 2, not 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.49,
            0.896,
            0.512,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "13"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.092,
            0.887,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": "2. For each of the kernel choices below, find the decision boundary diagram (on the next page) that best matches. In these diagrams, the brightness of a point represents the magnitude of the SVM output; red means positive output and blue means negative. The black circles are the negative training points and the white circles are the positive training points."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.2,
            0.479,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": "(a) Polynomial kernel, degree 2 : D"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.224,
            0.478,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "(b) Polynomial kernel, degree 3 : B"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.249,
            0.516,
            0.268
          ],
          "content": "(c) Radial basis kernel, sigma = 0.5 : A"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.273,
            0.517,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "(d) Radial basis kernel, sigma = 1.0 : C"
        },
        {
